\section{Local Setup and Notation}\label{sec:setup}

We model every document as a finite string of tokens. Formally this is the free monoid $(\Strings,\concat,\emptystr)$ recorded in Appendix~\ref{app:notation}, but in the main text we simply write $x=b_1\concat\cdots\concat b_k$ for the contiguous blocks that make up $x$. The task of interest is represented by a fixed oracle $\fstar:\Strings\to\Y$ whose output space $\Y$ can be another set of strings or an embedding space. Documents are random draws $X\sim\Law$ from some population law on $\Strings$.\footnote{The data-generating process $\Law$ may be a biased sample of the underlying state of the world. For instance, newspapers may selectively report certain types of events, or court opinions may reflect only cases that reach adjudication. Our analysis abstracts from this selection mechanism and takes the observed corpus as given; the oracle $\fstar$ extracts task-relevant information from whatever documents $\Law$ produces. Addressing selection bias in $\Law$ itself is outside the scope of this work.} None of the statements below depend on the specific form of $\Law$.

A summarizer $g:\Strings\rightsquigarrow\Strings$ may be deterministic or may use internal randomness (e.g., sampling temperature). When randomness is present, the statements below are understood after averaging over the seeds/call-counts used by $g$; in deployment, one can approximate these expectations by drawing multiple summaries and averaging their oracle distances. To keep the notation minimal we write $\E[\cdot]$ for all expectations and suppress the inner averaging. The quality of a summary is measured in oracle space: a (pseudo)metric $\metric:\Y\times\Y\to[0,\infty)$ induces the distortion
\[
D(z,x):=\metric\big(\fstar(z),\fstar(x)\big),
\]
so two strings are indistinguishable for our purposes when $D(z,x)=0$.

Downstream training only cares about the oracle. We formalize this with a simple conditional factorization: there is a population score $\sstar: \Strings\times \A\to\R$ (classification loss, reward, or any other signal) and a measurable $\bar Q$ such that

\begin{assumption}[Conditional Factorization]\label{ass:CF}
There exists a measurable function $\bar{Q}: \Y \times \A \to \mathbb{R}$ such that for all actions $a \in \A$,
\[
\E[\sstar(X, a) \mid \fstar(X)] = \bar{Q}(\fstar(X), a) \quad \text{almost surely}.
\]
\end{assumption}

This assumption covers standard classification settings (scores depend on the true label) and preference learning (scores depend on the task semantics), and it tolerates annotator noise because the conditioning is on $X$. Intuitively, once we know the task value $\fstar(X)$ there is no extra reward to be had from raw phrasing: if $\fstar(X)$ records the city, crowd size, and outcome of a protest, reviewers judging a summary care about those fields rather than the specific prose.

Because only $\fstar$ matters for decision making, the right notion of a ``good'' summary is one whose oracle matches that of the source. We separate the one-pass requirement from the ``do no harm when summarizing again'' requirement.

\begin{definition}[Oracle sufficiency in expectation]\label{def:expect-suff}
A (possibly stochastic) summarizer $g$ is \emph{$\fstar$‑sufficient in expectation}
if for every $x\in\Strings$,
\[
\E\!\big[\,\metric\big(\fstar(g(x)),\,\fstar(x)\big)\,\big]\;=\;0.
\]
For deterministic $g$, this reduces to the pointwise condition
$\metric\big(\fstar(g(x)),\fstar(x)\big)=0$ for all $x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Summary idempotence]\label{def:resum-stable}
The summarizer $g$ is \emph{summary-idempotent} (stable) if every string that already lies on the range of $g$ keeps its oracle after another call to $g$:
\[
\E\big[\metric\big(\fstar(g(Z)),\fstar(Z)\big)\mid Z\big]=0
\quad\text{for every random $Z$ supported on the range of $g$.}
\]
\end{definition}

Exact preservation is the special case in which the distortion is zero almost surely. We collect the corresponding shorthand.

\begin{definition}[Exact classes $\Gstar$ and $\Gstar_{\mathrm{stab}}$]\label{def:exact-suff-stable}
Write
\[
\Gstar:=\{g:\ \metric(\fstar(g(X)),\fstar(X))=0\text{ a.s.}\},
\qquad
\Gstar_{\mathrm{stab}}:=\{g\in\Gstar:\ g \text{ also satisfies Definition~\ref{def:resum-stable}}\}.
\]
\end{definition}

Doob–Dynkin factorization (Appendix~\ref{app:notation}) states that $g\in\Gstar$ iff the oracle can be recovered as a measurable function of $g(X)$, and stability is an additional property that constrains re-applications of $g$. Figure~\ref{fig:gstar} illustrates the equalities implied by membership in $\Gstar_{\mathrm{stab}}$.

When reasoning about concrete tasks, these conditions can be read literally. For example, if $\fstar$ extracts the set of people named in a multi-document dossier, Definition~\ref{def:expect-suff} says that every summary must yield exactly the same set of names, while Definition~\ref{def:resum-stable} says that repeatedly prompting the dossier with ``rewrite in canonical form'' cannot swap names in or out. The class $\Gstar_{\mathrm{stab}}$ simply collects summarizers that satisfy both properties.




In Figure \ref{fig:gstar} we graphically depict the equality requirements for a summarizer $g$ to be in $\Gstar$, specifically, sufficiency and summary idempotence. When $g\in \Gstar$, three paths yield the same answer: directly applying the oracle $\fstar(X)$, summarizing then applying the oracle $\fstar(g(X))$, and repeatedly summarizing then applying the oracle $\fstar(g(g(X)))$.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[node distance=2cm]
\node[doc] (X) {$X$};
\node[sum, right=of X] (gX) {$g(X)$};
\node[sum, right=of gX] (ggX) {$g(g(X))$};

\node[op, below=of X] (fX) {$\fstar(X)$};
\node[op, below=of gX] (fgX) {$\fstar(g(X))$};
\node[op, below=of ggX] (fggX) {$\fstar(g(g(X)))$};

\draw[->, dotted] (X) -- node[above] {} (gX);
\draw[->, dotted] (gX) -- node[above] {} (ggX);
\draw[->] (X) -- node[left] {} (fX);
\draw[->] (gX) -- node[left] {} (fgX);
\draw[->] (ggX) -- node[left] {} (fggX);

\draw[<->, dashed] (fX) -- node[below] {\tiny $\metric$-equal if $g \in \Gstar$} (fgX);
