\caption{Oracle-preserving normal form: one summarization preserves the task-relevant information, and re-summarizing an already-summarized text leaves it unchanged.}
\label{fig:gstar}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Document Decomposition}

Long documents routinely exceed LLM context windows, so we first split $x$ into contiguous blocks $(b_1,\ldots,b_k)$ and then summarize them hierarchically. Any full binary tree whose leaves are $(b_1,\ldots,b_k)$ serves as a \emph{reduction tree}: the two children of an internal node are concatenated and summarized again, and the process repeats until a single top summary remains. This description is deliberately agnostic about the global ``hierarchical summarization'' literature; we only rely on the realized schedule that a deployment actually runs. We denote the one-pass output by $Z^{(1)}(x)$ and write $Z^{(R+1)}(x):=g(Z^{(R)}(x))$ for optional ``normalization'' rounds.

The informal description above already captures most deployed systems. Analysts decide on a block size, specify how to merge blocks (balanced, left-deep, folder-by-folder, etc.), ask an LLM to summarize each leaf, and continue merging until a top summary is produced. The key point for our notation is that every intermediate summary sits on a node of the realized tree $T$; nothing else about the order or batching will matter for the theorems.

We formalize this “summarize, concatenate, summarize again’’ recipe in Algorithm~\ref{alg:las}. The only inputs are the base document $x_0$, the tree $T$ that captures the realized merge schedule, and the desired number of post-processing rounds.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Locally Audited Summarization}\label{alg:las}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\STATE \textbf{Input:} base document $x_0$, full binary tree $T$ on contiguous blocks $(b_1,\ldots,b_k)$, number of rounds $R$
\STATE Partition $x_0$ into contiguous blocks $(b_1,\ldots,b_k)$ that each fit a single call to $g$
\FOR{each leaf $b_i$ of $T$}
    \STATE Evaluate $g(b_i)$
\ENDFOR
\FOR{each internal node $u$ of $T$ traversed bottom-up}
    \STATE $g(u) \gets g\big(g(u_L)\concat g(u_R)\big)$
\ENDFOR
\STATE $Z^{(1)}(x) \gets g(\mathrm{root}(T))$
\FOR{$r=1$ to $R-1$}
    \STATE $Z^{(r+1)}(x) \gets g\big(Z^{(r)}(x)\big)$
\ENDFOR
\STATE \textbf{return} $Z^{(R)}(x)$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

Some deployments also aggregate within larger “folds’’ (e.g., summarize each issue of a magazine and then summarize across issues). If $F_1,\ldots,F_J$ are contiguous folds of $(b_1,\ldots,b_k)$, we may pick arbitrary trees $T_1,\ldots,T_J$ within folds and an arbitrary over-fold tree $\widehat T$; folding each level with the same $g$ fits neatly into the notation developed below and is covered by our guarantees.

Figure \ref{fig:hierarchical_summarization} shows the resulting “summarize, concatenate, summarize again” pipeline, which is the object we audit in the next subsection.



\begin{figure}[t]
